ers. Most companies include these amounts in Box 1 of your Form W\u20112 and withhold tax (sometimes they also \u201cgross up\u201d to cover estimated taxes). If it\u2019s on your W\u20112, it\u2019s already part of your 2023 taxable wages and you don\u2019t report it separately. If it\u2019s not on your W\u20112, it may appear on a Form 1042\u2011S (for nonresidents), but it\u2019s still taxable U.S. compensation tied to your employment. There\u2019s typically no treaty exemption for employer moving reimbursements.\\n\\n2) 2023 U.S. tax residency (Substantial Presence Test)\\n- You are a resident for 2023 if: (a) you were in the U.S. at least 31 days during 2023, and (b) your weighted days meet 183: all 2023 days + 1/3 of 2022 days + 1/6 of 2021 days \u2265 183. If you had no U.S. days in 2021\u20132022, then you\u2019d need \u2265183 days in 2023 alone to be a resident.\\n- If you arrived mid\u20112023 and have <183 days in 2023, you\u2019re generally a nonresident for 2023. However, you may be eligible for the First\u2011Year Choice (if you had a 31\u2011day consecutive presence in 2023 and were present at least 75% of the days from that start date through 12/31). That makes you a dual\u2011status resident for part of the year, which can affect what income is taxed as U.S.\u2011source vs worldwide.\\n\\nWhat to do now\\n- Please confirm your exact 2023 U.S. entry/exit dates and whether your W\u20112 shows the relocation amount in Box 1 (often noted as \u201cRelocation\u201d in the earnings detail). If you didn\u2019t receive a W\u20112, please let me know if you received a Form 1042\u2011S.\\n- Pending that confirmation, you can assume the relocation reimbursement is taxable for 2023. If resident (or dual\u2011status), it will be in your W\u20112 wages. If nonresident, it\u2019s reportable on Form 1040\u2011NR (or via W\u20112 wages depending on how the employer reported it).\\n\\nNote: States may have their own nuances on moving reimbursements. Once I know your state of residence in 2023, I can confirm the state treatment as well.\\n\\nIf you share your 2023 travel dates, I\u2019ll compute the residency result and confirm exactly how it should appear on your return. Thanks!', npc_name='all', action='chat'), ErrorObservation(content='The action has not been executed.', observation='error', error_id='')], inputs={}, outputs={}, agent_state=<AgentState.ERROR: 'error'>, resume_state=None, traffic_control_state=<TrafficControlState.NORMAL: 'normal'>, metrics=Metrics({'accumulated_cost': 0.18872650000000002, 'costs': [{'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.005255000000000001, 'timestamp': 1763532926.474965}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.0018615000000000003, 'timestamp': 1763532928.352179}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.0026235, 'timestamp': 1763532933.5725987}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.00402925, 'timestamp': 1763532946.6608312}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.0018277500000000002, 'timestamp': 1763532949.9345083}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.0123455, 'timestamp': 1763532979.8531654}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.02621025, 'timestamp': 1763533048.3823578}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.012932750000000002, 'timestamp': 1763533096.5784252}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.00121325, 'timestamp': 1763533128.5721846}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.00125675, 'timestamp': 1763533129.8081794}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.02480775, 'timestamp': 1763533178.6039436}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.005255000000000001, 'timestamp': 1763532926.474965}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.0018615000000000003, 'timestamp': 1763532928.352179}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.0026235, 'timestamp': 1763532933.5725987}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.00402925, 'timestamp': 1763532946.6608312}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.0018277500000000002, 'timestamp': 1763532949.9345083}, {'model': 'openai/gpt-5', 'cost': 0.0123455, 'timestamp': 1763532979.8531654}, {'model': 'o